hi there steve kaufman here again uh in  this fourth  in this series of uh  steve summer sessions where i ask you to  send in your questions  uh about language learning or about link  or  and also i should add in any language  that i can answer i'm quite happy to  answer questions in languages  other than english as long as i speak  them  the questions i've asked you to submit  them through twitter  at hashtags ask steve  now today i want to answer some  questions that i've received that relate  to fluency  questions such as  what is fluency how do you achieve  fluency how do you achieve fluency in  the country where the language is spoken  not in the country where the language is  spoken and so forth okay  my definition of fluency is  it's a bit like um  you know are you fit  okay you don't have to be a bodybuilder  you don't have to be a marathon runner  in order to be fit  uh  i think fluency is a matter of feeling  comfortable in the language  comfortable that you understand most  situations comfortable that you can  basically express yourself in most  situations but it doesn't mean that  you're perfect it doesn't mean that your  accent is  you know  easily mistaken for a native speaker  it means that you make grammatical  mistakes it means that you occasionally  can't find the right word uh it means  that you may very well have a pronounced  accent  you know my father who was born in  czechoslovakia  he had a  very identical  accent for  most of the 30 years that he lived in  canada but he was absolutely fluent in  english did business in english and we  have all met people who are very fluent  in english and who have certain turns of  phrase that are still  more or less you know reflect their  uh country of origin  uh or who have accents and who make  mistakes so fluent means to be  comfortable in the language  and so in my own case so then the next  question is can you become fluent  if you don't live in the country  where the language is spoken and of  course the answer is yes  i was on a speaking tour in china about  eight years ago  and i spoke to students at various  universities there including the um i  think it was called the beijing y yes  yeah yeah  and uh also in uh  near shanghai the  uh hua dong shira fandasia or something  or in jejung i think it was the young  dasia in hungary  and i met all kinds of students who were  excellent in english japanese french and  so forth so these were obviously  students who had dedicated themselves to  learning the language so i think it is  possible but it takes an awful lot of  work to to achieve real fluency not in  the country where the language is spoken  uh it's a lot easier to do in the  language in the country the language is  spoken but being in the country where  the language is spoken doesn't ensure  that you're going to become fluent i  lived in japan for nine years i learned  japanese most foreigners there didn't  bother or maybe they tried but they  didn't succeed  you know we know lots of immigrants in  different countries who lived there for  20 or 30 years and never learned to  speak the language properly  so  how do you become fluent all right i  believe that it takes a lot of work  regardless where you are it takes a lot  of the same kinds of things that i talk  about all the time a lot of time with  the language a positive attitude towards  the language wanting to learn the  language liking the language uh  in my case a lot of input type  activities a lot of listening and  reading  using things that are of interest to me  building up my  basic comprehension abilities and my  vocabulary so that you build up your  potential to become fluent however to be  genuinely fluent  uh and remember this means to be  comfortable in the language i'll just  digress for a second every so often i'll  put up a video in some language and  people say well you're no good you make  mistakes and and someone said to be  really fluent in a language you have to  be able to  uh say things like  how do i time my shoelaces i can't  remember there's a long list i think it  was a commenter here at youtube  of very obscure things that a native  speaker can say i know how to say time  my shoelaces in english but i don't  necessarily know how to say that in  other languages that i speak quite well  it doesn't come up if it doesn't come up  very often you're not going to be used  to saying it you can't be you're not  going to go and study a whole bunch of  possible obscure scenarios you learn  basic vocabulary through following your  interests and the first or second time i  have to express myself on how to tie my  shoelaces i'll know how to tie my  shoelaces it's not important  fluency is just being comfortable in  most situations  so to become comfortable in most  situations i believe eventually you have  to speak and you have to speak a lot  because fluency implies not only that  you understand and you can read fluency  implies that you can express yourself  not necessarily in writing because not  every situation requires writing and  there's lots of people who are very  fluent  uh in languages that you know where  they don't write i don't know uh in  i i added that in ethiopia people are  quite multilingual but i'm sure they  don't write in all the different  languages that they speak so writing to  my mind is not a condition for fluency  uh  it may be a  a skill that one should train and it may  be required for certain jobs but it's  not an absolute condition of fluency but  speaking comfortably is so in order to  speak comfortably you have to speak a  lot it's difficult to create a situation  where you speak a lot if you're away  from the country where the language is  spoken those students in china i'm sure  they have lots of opportunity to  practice their language they were fully  dedicated to the task but for the  average learner like me who puts in an  hour to a day i can't simply suspend my  normal professional and social  activities in order to look for romanian  speakers or korean speakers and  socialize with them all the time it's  just not practical  so  but eventually if you want to become  fluent you have to expo you have to  speak a lot okay so what does that mean  if i summarize it means that uh  you know you you should invest a lot of  time and effort into building up your  passive capacity in the language which i  have done in say 13 languages  but if i look at the languages that i'm  most fluent in  english obviously french and japanese  those are the languages where i have  lived  in the country where the language is  spoken and so i am much more fluent in  those than any others  the next on my list is mandarin because  i studied it full time like those  students in china i was at it seven  eight hours a day but i lived in hong  kong which is not a mandarin speaking  environment so i didn't you know have  that chance to bring it up to the same  level as my japanese  however i have a very good potential  level in mandarin  i still call myself fluent although i'm  not as good as in japanese but if i were  to live in china for three months  i have a sufficient  grounding through my massive input  activities that i could bring myself up  to quite a high level now next on my  list let's say is spanish i have never  lived in a spanish-speaking country i  have spent at most one or two weeks in a  spanish-speaking country on a number of  different occasions when i'm there my  spanish improves when i go away and i  don't speak so often my spanish  basically  goes down a notch although the potential  is still there and then as you go on to  my languages you know german swedish  italian russian czech i feel that in all  of them including romanian  uh where i just visited for three four  days if i had the opportunity to live  there for let's say three months  in any of those countries russia  czech republic and so forth italy  that i could bring my language ability  way up because i would be speaking all  the time so  uh when we talk about fluency i don't  think it's possible even for luca and  and richard simcott who are outstanding  polyglots they can't have  all of their languages you know totally  fine tuned so that at the drop of the  hat of a hat they can just  wow just completely  feel comfortable in the language they  may have a handful of languages where  they are very good and comfortable  easily but then others where they are  potentially fluent and i would still  call them fluent  but  it would take a week two weeks in the  country to really be  comfortable  so  you know  how do you get the fluency it's the same  as how you learn languages in my opinion  a lot of input activities following your  interest building up your vocabulary but  eventually a lot of speaking  a lot of speaking is more easily done  in the country where the language is  spoken but even if you are in the  country where the language is spoken if  all you do is speak  if you don't put that effort into  increasing your vocabulary broadening  the range of your comprehension then  you'll be like these immigrants who can  speak at a certain level but never  really get beyond that level so even in  the country where the language is spoken  you have to put that effort into your  input activities  developing that ability to notice the  language the pronunciation the  structures you have to put the effort in  if you're not in that country where the  language is spoken you would have to put  in a lot of time  uh not only in input but eventually in  talking to people via skype or otherwise  in order to achieve  fluency but the final word is that  fluency  is not  equivalent to being perfect you don't  have to speak with no noticeable accent  you don't have to speak with no mistakes  you don't have to be able to express  yourself on every single  conceivable situation including how to  tie your shoelaces  there will be situations where you don't  understand  you may be fluent and still miss some  things while watching a movie you can  still be fluent so that's my definition  of fluency and that i hope i've touched  on some of the other questions that  related to how do we achieve fluency and  how do we achieve fluency both in the  country where the language is spoken and  away from the country where the language  is spoken so thank you very much  and i look forward to your questions and  comments bye for now
 

